(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-2-ocular-trauma-quiz"]={"title":"Ocular Trauma Quiz 1 — CMS I Exam 2","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 2","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"Which type of retinal detachment is the most common?","choices":["Congenital","Rhegmatogenous","Traction","Exudative","Combined traction and exudative"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Full-thickness retinal breaks let liquefied vitreous behind the retina. — CMS I Ocular Trauma - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"In a true blowout fracture, which wall gives way most often?","choices":["The posterior wall","The superior orbital rim","The orbital floor","The lateral wall","The orbital roof"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Raised orbital pressure blows out the floor most frequently, or the medial wall. — CMS I Ocular Trauma - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 40"},{"q":"A patient with a basilar skull fracture has a cerebrospinal fluid leak. What follows?","choices":["Repeat computed tomography in one week","Neurosurgery consult and admission","Discharge with outpatient follow-up","Ophthalmology consult and observation","Antibiotics alone, then discharge"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The leak is what forces both. — CMS I Ocular Trauma - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 47"},{"q":"Which instruction must a patient with a corneal abrasion never be given?","choices":["Return if symptoms worsen","Consider patching for comfort","Avoid rubbing the eye","Take home topical anaesthetic drops for pain","Use the topical antibiotic as prescribed"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. They delay healing, mask worsening symptoms and can cause a corneal ulcer. — CMS I Ocular Trauma - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"What is the limitation of computed tomography of the orbits in basilar skull fracture?","choices":["It cannot show a cerebrospinal fluid leak at all","It is contraindicated after head trauma","It requires contrast that may be nephrotoxic","It cannot be done in an unconscious patient","The fracture is not always evident on it"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct. A normal scan does not exclude it. — CMS I Ocular Trauma - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 47"},{"q":"Why is ophthalmology consulted for a true blowout fracture even without entrapment?","choices":["Because every fracture needs operative repair","Because the optic nerve is always damaged","To arrange spectacles for the diplopia","To exclude a basilar skull fracture","About 30% sustain a significant globe injury"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct. The globe has to be assessed. — CMS I Ocular Trauma - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"Over what period does most rebleeding after hyphema occur?","choices":["Rebleeding is not a recognised complication","The first 72 hours","The first 24 hours only","Between one and two weeks","After the first month"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. That is when a secondary haemorrhage is most likely. — CMS I Ocular Trauma - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"Why is a basilar skull fracture often identified from indirect signs?","choices":["It often produces no symptoms of its own","The fracture is always visible on plain films","It never causes bleeding","Patients are always unconscious","The signs appear within minutes"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The indirect signs are what give it away. — CMS I Ocular Trauma - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 44"},{"q":"What proportion of full-thickness lid lacerations come with a corneal laceration or globe rupture?","choices":["About one in ten","About one quarter","Almost none","About two thirds","About one in twenty"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Always look underneath a full-thickness lid cut. — CMS I Ocular Trauma - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"A patient has a periorbital haematoma with no history of injury. Which cause should be sought?","choices":["Contact lens overwear","A previous hyphema","Anticoagulants, eye surgery or orbital varices","A corneal abrasion earlier in the week","Seasonal allergy"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. It is not always traumatic. — CMS I Ocular Trauma - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"Why may a facial laceration be left open for 24 hours before closure?","choices":["The face is highly vascular","It reduces the need for antibiotics","It allows swelling to resolve first","It improves the cosmetic result in every case","It is required by tetanus prophylaxis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. That vascularity is what permits the delay. — CMS I Ocular Trauma - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"Which history point matters because surgery may follow?","choices":["The patient's usual spectacle prescription","Whether the patient wears contact lenses at night","Family history of glaucoma","When the patient last ate and drank","Which eye is dominant"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Nil-by-mouth status shapes the anaesthetic plan. — CMS I Ocular Trauma - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"A patient has an orbital contusion after a blow to the face. What must be excluded?","choices":["Allergic conjunctivitis","Retinoblastoma","Brain trauma","Thyroid eye disease","Chronic open-angle glaucoma"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The force that bruised the orbit reaches the brain. — CMS I Ocular Trauma - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"Which event usually precedes a rhegmatogenous retinal detachment?","choices":["Posterior vitreous detachment","Central retinal artery occlusion","Acute angle closure","Optic neuritis","Corneal ulceration"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The vitreous separates first, then traction opens a retinal break. — CMS I Ocular Trauma - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"When is intraocular pressure NOT measured in a patient with hyphema?","choices":["When the hyphema fills less than half the chamber","When the patient is under sixteen","When the injury was more than a day ago","Pressure is measured in every case","When a penetrating globe injury is suspected"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct. Pressing on an open globe risks extruding its contents. — CMS I Ocular Trauma - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"Within what period must a patient with a retinal detachment see ophthalmology?","choices":["Within 2 to 3 days","At the next routine clinic","Only if the vision worsens","Within 24 hours","Within one week"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The referral is immediate. — CMS I Ocular Trauma - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"A 24-year-old man arrives after a workshop explosion with an obviously injured right eye and noisy breathing. What comes first?","choices":["Visual acuity in both eyes","A shield over the injured eye","Computed tomography of the orbits","Ophthalmology consultation","Airway, breathing and circulation"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct. The eye waits until the patient is stable. — CMS I Ocular Trauma - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 3"},{"q":"Which history makes globe rupture more likely at lower force?","choices":["Seasonal allergic conjunctivitis","Previous corneal abrasion","Previous intraocular surgery","A history of migraine","Long-standing myopia alone"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Old incisions leave a permanent weak point. — CMS I Ocular Trauma - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"Why is oral aminocaproic acid used in hyphema?","choices":["It dilates the pupil to rest the ciliary body","It slows clot breakdown","It lowers the intraocular pressure","It reverses anticoagulation","It prevents secondary infection"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. It is an antifibrinolytic. — CMS I Ocular Trauma - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"A patient with ocular trauma is intoxicated and cannot give a history. Where should the account come from?","choices":["The examination findings alone","The transferring service's diagnosis","Family, friends or bystanders","The medical record alone","Waiting until the patient sobers"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The circumstance of injury still has to be established. — CMS I Ocular Trauma - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"When does a corneal foreign body need ophthalmology rather than removal at the bedside?","choices":["When the patient wears contact lenses","When there is a rust ring","When it has been present more than a day","When the patient is a child","When it may have passed through the cornea"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct. That is an open globe, not a surface foreign body. — CMS I Ocular Trauma - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"A rust ring remains after a metallic foreign body is removed. How is it dealt with?","choices":["A cotton-tipped applicator","Leaving it to resorb","Topical chelating drops","A burr tip drill","Repeated irrigation with saline"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. That is how a rust ring comes out. — CMS I Ocular Trauma - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"A 30-year-old has a distorted pupil pulled toward a corneal wound, a flat anterior chamber and a soft eye. Which diagnosis fits?","choices":["Open globe injury","Corneal abrasion","Traumatic iritis","Subconjunctival haemorrhage","Orbital contusion"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Those three findings together define a full-thickness defect. — CMS I Ocular Trauma - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"A patient struck in the eye has blood layered in the anterior chamber. What is this, and what else must be considered?","choices":["Corneal blood staining","Hyphema, which can itself be a sign of open globe","Hyphema, which excludes an open globe","Subconjunctival haemorrhage over the iris","Vitreous haemorrhage seen through the pupil"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. It is not always an isolated injury. — CMS I Ocular Trauma - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"A partial-thickness lid laceration meets none of the referral criteria. How is it handled?","choices":["Left open permanently to granulate","Closed with tissue adhesive and no follow-up","Referred to plastic surgery routinely","Repair there, ophthalmology in 2 to 3 days","Immediate transfer for operative repair"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. It does not need the specialist to close it. — CMS I Ocular Trauma - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"Which examination shows the extent of a corneal abrasion?","choices":["Slit lamp fluorescein","Tonometry","Gonioscopy","Dilated fundoscopy","Ocular ultrasound"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Fluorescein stains the exposed basement membrane. — CMS I Ocular Trauma - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"In which population is ocular trauma the leading cause of monocular blindness, and why does that shape counselling?","choices":["Adolescent girls, so sports goggles are the message","Men over seventy, so driving assessment is the message","Young adult men, so eye protection matters most","Elderly women, so fall prevention is the message","Infants, so childproofing is the message"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. That is the group in which it leads. — CMS I Ocular Trauma - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"A traction retinal detachment is most commonly caused by what?","choices":["Age-related macular degeneration","Retinitis pigmentosa","Uveal melanoma","Blunt ocular trauma alone","Proliferative diabetic retinopathy"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct. Fibrovascular tissue pulls the retina off. — CMS I Ocular Trauma - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"A patient has numbness of the cheek after an orbital fracture. Which nerve is injured?","choices":["The trochlear nerve","The infraorbital nerve","The optic nerve","The oculomotor nerve","The facial nerve"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Infraorbital numbness is a recognised finding. — CMS I Ocular Trauma - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 41"},{"q":"Which set of measures accompanies the shield in a suspected open globe?","choices":["Antiemetics, analgesia, tetanus and computed tomography","A mydriatic, tonometry and dilated fundoscopy of the retina","Copious irrigation and a pressure patch","Topical anaesthetic drops to take home","Oral corticosteroids and observation"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Keeping the patient from straining protects the eye. — CMS I Ocular Trauma - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 17"}]};
